Problem 1:

FIRST PART:
- The sequence of nodes generated by preorder tree traversal is:
A - B - E - H - I - F - C - G - D

- The sequence of nodes generated by breadth-first tree traversal is:
A - B - C -D - E - F - G - H - I 

- The sequence of nodes generated by post tree traversal is:
H - E - I - F - B - C - G - D - A

SECOND PART (BINARY TREE):
The sequence of nodes generated by in-order tree traversal:
D - G - B - E - A - C - H -F - I

For problems 2 and 3:
Algorithm: 

Problem 2: Fewest elements whose sum is greater than T.

## The Algorithm:
To get a sum greater than 'T' with as few numbers as possible, I use the biggest numbers first. I sort the array from largest to smallest, add the numbers one by one, and stop as soon as the running sum is greater than 'T'. The number of values I added is the answer.
This is correct because for any fixed number of elements k, the largest sum you can get from k elements is the sum of the k largest elements. 
## How the code works:
The loop takes the numbers one at a time. current_sum is the running total and count is how many numbers I have used so far. As soon as current_sum > target , the function returns count.

Problem 3: Counting right triangles

## The Algorithm:
The direct way is to take every set of three points and test whether one of the angles is 90 degrees. However this is too slow and takes O(n**3) and the assignment asks for a solution in less than 30 seconds for a large input.
What I did: 
Every right triangle has exactly one 90 degrees corner. So I pick each point 'P' in turn as the possible right-angle corner, and count the right triangles that have their right angle at 'P'. Adding these counts up over all points counts every triangle once. 
For a fixed 'P', a right angle at 'P' needs two other points 'Q' and 'R' so that the direction from 'P' to 'Q' is perpendicular to the direction from 'P' to 'R'. So for each 'P', I:
- work out the direction from P to every other point,
- group the points by direction (points on the same ray from 'P' form one group).
- for each group, find the group in the perpendicular direction and multiply the two group sizes.
## How the code works:
Point 'i' is the corner 'P'. 'freq' is a dictionary that counts how many points lie in each direction from P. It is created again for every pivot.
(dx, dy) is the vector from 'P' to the other point'Q'. I divide both parts by their greatest common divisor 'g' to get the simplest form. This way, every point on the same ray from 'P' ends up with the same key. 
For every direction (dx, dy) in 'freq', I look for 'perp_vec' = (-dy, dx), which is the same vector rotated 90 degrees counter-clockwise. If it is in 'freq', any point from the first group together with any point from the second group makes a right angle at P, so I add 'count * freq[perp_vec]' to 'total_triangles'.
Nothing is counted twice or by mistake because:
- A triangle has only one right angle, so it is counted only at its own corner 'P'.
- For two perpendicular directions A and B at 'P', either B is A rotated 90 degrees counter-clockwise, or A is B rotated 90 degrees counter-clockwise, but never both. The code only looks at the counter-clockwise rotation so each pair of directions is counted exactly once.
## Time complexity: There are two nested loops , so about n(n-1) steps. 'gcd' and the dictionary lookups take constant time on average, so we end up with a total time of O(n**2).



